<section xml:id="sec-section-DRV" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Discrete RVs</title>


  
  <introduction>
    <p>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- EXERCISE 1: DISCRETE PROBABILITY SYSTEM                              -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-int-discrete-prob">
    <title>Interactive Analysis: Discrete Asset Metrics</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        When evaluating market assets, analysts utilize probabilistic frameworks to model the time remaining on a product's patent after deducting regulatory delays. A biomedical firm tracks the actual commercial patent life, <m>Y</m>, rounded to the nearest integer year, for a recent portfolio of medical software systems. The probability distribution of <m>Y</m> is given in the table below:
      </p>
      <table>
        <title>Discrete Distribution for Software Patent Lifespan <m>Y</m></title>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Actual Patent Life, <m>y</m> (years)</cell>
            <cell>Probability, <m>P(y)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>4</cell><cell>0.05</cell></row>
          <row><cell>5</cell><cell>0.10</cell></row>
          <row><cell>6</cell><cell>0.20</cell></row>
          <row><cell>7</cell><cell>0.35</cell></row>
          <row><cell>8</cell><cell>0.20</cell></row>
          <row><cell>9</cell><cell>0.10</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </statement>
    


    <!-- Part A: Interactive Multiple Choice -->
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Calculate the probability that a randomly chosen software portfolio piece features a commercial patent lifespan of <strong>at least 7 years</strong>; that is, find <m>P(Y \ge 7)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>0.35</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect. This is only the isolated probability of exactly 7 years: <m>P(Y = 7)</m>. The phrase <q>at least 7</q> requires including all outcomes greater than or equal to 7.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>0.65</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct! The event <m>Y \ge 7</m> aggregates the mutually exclusive states where <m>y = 7, 8,</m> or <m>9</m>. Summing these yields: <m>0.35 + 0.20 + 0.10 = 0.65</m>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>0.30</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect. This corresponds to the structural complement <m>P(Y \lt 6)</m>, rather than the subset equal to or exceeding 7 years.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>0.95</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect. Double check your summation boundaries; this configuration over-includes lower years that fall out of bounds.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Interactive Fill-in Numeric Entry -->
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Calculate the expected value, <m>\mu = E(Y)</m>, of the commercial patent life for these software products (in years). Input your final value as a exact decimal.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">6.85</test>
          <feedback>
            <p>Excellent! The expected value is the probability-weighted sum of all outcomes: <m>4(0.05) + 5(0.10) + 6(0.20) + 7(0.35) + 8(0.20) + 9(0.10) = 0.20 + 0.50 + 1.20 + 2.45 + 1.60 + 0.90 = 6.85</m> years.</p>
          </feedback>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test>6.5</test>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. It appears you calculated an unweighted average of the years rather than applying their respective probabilities.</p></feedback>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  
</section>